<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.12.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01081</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.12.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ca97cd30-7030-48a8-ab25-5360c7d1aab9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="daaa1a96-0e9c-459b-946e-30da9433ff9e" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="445b191e-c8fd-46a7-b4ba-3d8d1acdf66f" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;30&quot;, &quot;90&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;20&quot;, &quot;30&quot;, &quot;50&quot;, &quot;90&quot;, &quot;100&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>
          The cost for an upcoming field trip is $15 per student. The cost of the field trip \(C\), in dollars, is a function of the number of students \(x\).
          <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> possible outputs for the function defined by \(C(x)=15x\).
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 30, 90</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.12.1-4.12.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q2-->
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e3786363-f2c0-4c5e-9873-59843229d9d0" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;3&quot;, &quot;0.5&quot;, &quot;48&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;3&quot;, &quot;0.5&quot;, &quot;48&quot;, &quot;-6&quot;, &quot;0&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A rectangle has an area of \(24 cm^2\). Function \(f\) gives the length of the rectangle, in centimeters, when the width is \(w\) cm.<emphasis>Select three</emphasis> that apply.
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 3, 0.5, and 48 are possible inputs. Not possible: -6, 0.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Not quite right. Consider the factors of 24 to find possible widths.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="3e4feb7c-ba40-4446-a165-e68e84084e4b" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\(\\left(\\frac12,\\frac18\\right)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((4,64)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-1,-1)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((-1,-1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-2,8)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((3,9)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(\\left(\\frac12,\\frac18\\right)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((4,64)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((1,-1)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> possible input-output pairs for the function \(y=x^3\).</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!  \((-1,-1)\), \(\left(\frac12,\frac18\right)\), \((4,64)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="93103872-a3b8-4df7-9763-85f8e25df230" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\(f(3)\\) &gt; \\(0\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(g(-1)=0.8\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(3)\\) &gt; \\(0\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(3)\\) &gt; \\(5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(g(-1)=0.8\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(0)=g(0)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Two functions are defined by the equations \(f(x)=5−0.2x\) and \(g(x)=0.2(x+5)\). <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> possible statements that are true about the functions.
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.3</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="47195a33-5c1b-46a6-8283-458cc16086e9" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Is the relation graphed below a function?</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="The figure shows the graph of some points on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 6 to 6. The points (negative 3, 4), (negative 3, negative 1), (0, negative 3), (2, 3), (4, negative 1), and (4, negative 3)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/944cfd8f63cb30c6cde5373d42c53e3103f39d39"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! No, this is not a function because 4 maps to both -1 and −3.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="77029a4d-3998-48ee-adfb-b3ccd2b2c34e" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Is the relation graphed below a function?</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="The figure shows the graph of some points on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 6 to 6. The points (negative 3, 4), (negative 3, negative 4), (negative 2, 0), (negative 1, 3), (1, 5), and (4, negative 2)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c26a999a0d0a7ad911c353f0086dbd924ea0ba97"/></media></figure></para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! no; This is not a function because −3 maps to both 4 and −4. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.12.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q7a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="783c6eec-081f-41b1-9e43-078fa58bfd52" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="0">
    <problem>
      <para>Use the following scenario for questions 7 - 9.</para>
      <blockquote>
        <para>For a fundraiser, Chi and her class are having a talent show and charging $7 a ticket. There are 100 seats in the theater. The function \(T\), gives the total for selling \(n\) tickets and is represented by \(T(n)=7n\).</para>
      </blockquote>
      <para>Enter the minimum domain value.
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The minimum domain value is 0</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q7b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="707cc6fb-1ff4-4f84-b98b-3ea6f7155506" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="100">
    <problem>
      <para>Enter the maximum domain value.

        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The maximum domain value is 100.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="de1ba9ff-c4e5-45b0-a9b9-c2d4d207c08b" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Is 30 a possible output in the range?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! No; 30 is not a possible value in the range because all values of the range must be multiples of 7. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.12.5.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution>
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
  </div>
</div></section>
</content>
